\title{The Molien Invariant Is the Cyclic Shadow\\ of the Burnside Mark Vector}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For a finite group $G$ acting on a finite set $X$ there are two classical subgroup-indexed
invariants: the \emph{Burnside mark vector} $H \mapsto |X^H|$, recording the number of points
fixed by all of $H$, and the \emph{Molien invariant}
$H \mapsto \frac{1}{|H|}\sum_{h\in H}|X^h|$, the subgroup-wise average of the permutation
character. Conjecture D10 asserts that these two invariants agree up to a scalar. We settle
the conjecture completely. On the positive side we prove the Averaging Theorem: the Molien
invariant at $H$ equals the average over $h\in H$ of the marks at the cyclic subgroups
$\langle h\rangle$, so the mark vector always determines the Molien invariant by a linear map
independent of $X$. As a sharp converse, if two finite $G$-sets have equal permutation
characters then their marks agree at every cyclic subgroup; hence over a group all of whose
subgroups are cyclic the Molien data determines the whole mark vector with scaling factor
exactly $1$. On the negative side we exhibit, for the Klein four group $V=(\ZZ/2)^2$, two
six-element $V$-sets with identical Molien invariants at every subgroup whose mark vectors are
not proportional, refuting the conjecture; and we generalise this to an infinite family, one
for each prime $p$, over $E=(\ZZ/p)^2$, comparing $\bigsqcup_{\ell\in\PP^1(\FF_p)}E/\ell$ with
$E\sqcup(p\ \text{fixed points})$. The obstruction is located exactly: the averaging samples
only cyclic subgroups, so a rank-two elementary abelian subgroup is precisely what breaks the
conjecture. We also record the comparison $|X^H|\le\Mol_X(H)$ with its equality case, the
Burnside orbit-counting identity in this normalisation, and the divisibility
$|H|\mid\sum_{h\in H}|X^h|$; applying the last to colouring representations yields the
necklace congruence $n\mid\sum_{a\in\ZZ/n}k^{\gcd(n,a)}$ and Fermat's little theorem
$k^p\equiv k \pmod p$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Let $G$ be a finite group. The category of finite $G$-sets is controlled by two classical,
closely related linear invariants.

The first is the \emph{table of marks}, introduced by Burnside: for a finite $G$-set $X$ and a
subgroup $H\le G$ it records the cardinality $|X^H|$ of the set of points fixed by every
element of $H$. Two finite $G$-sets are isomorphic if and only if they have equal mark
vectors, so the mark vector is a complete invariant and gives a coordinate system on the
Burnside ring $A(G)$.

The second is the \emph{permutation character} $g\mapsto|X^g|$, or equivalently its
subgroup-wise averages
\[
  \Mol_X(H) \;=\; \frac{1}{|H|}\sum_{h\in H}|X^{h}|,
\]
the constant-term data of the Molien series of the permutation representation restricted to
$H$ and, by Burnside's orbit-counting lemma, the number of $H$-orbits of $X$. We call
$H\mapsto\Mol_X(H)$ the \emph{Molien invariant}.

\begin{conjecture}[D10]\label{conj:D10}
The Molien invariant is exactly the Burnside mark vector modulo scaling: if two finite
$G$-sets $X,Y$ satisfy $\Mol_X(H)=\Mol_Y(H)$ for all $H\le G$, then there is a constant
$c\in\QQ$ with $|X^H| = c\,|Y^H|$ for all $H\le G$.
\end{conjecture}

We settle Conjecture~\ref{conj:D10} in both directions and identify the exact structural
boundary of its validity:
\begin{enumerate}
\item marks always determine Molien, by the Averaging Theorem
      $\Mol_X(H)=\frac{1}{|H|}\sum_{h\in H}|X^{\langle h\rangle}|$
      (Theorem~\ref{thm:avg}), which samples only \emph{cyclic} subgroups;
\item Molien determines marks when all subgroups are cyclic, with scaling factor $1$
      (Theorem~\ref{thm:cyclic} and Corollary~\ref{cor:cyclic});
\item Conjecture~\ref{conj:D10} is false (Theorem~\ref{thm:D10false}), by a six-point
      counterexample over the Klein four group, and false for every prime
      (Theorem~\ref{thm:ea});
\item the comparison $|X^H|\le\Mol_X(H)$ holds with equality iff $H$ acts trivially
      (Theorems~\ref{thm:cmp} and \ref{thm:eqcase});
\item the divisibility $|H|\mid\sum_{h\in H}|X^h|$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:dvd}), applied to
      colouring actions, yields the necklace congruences and Fermat's little theorem
      (Section~\ref{sec:arith}).
\end{enumerate}

The moral is contained in the first item: \emph{averaging over a subgroup can only see the
cyclic subgroups it contains}.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout $G$ is a group, $X$ and $Y$ are finite $G$-sets, and $H\le G$ is a finite subgroup.

\begin{definition}[Fixed-point count]
For $g\in G$, $\fix_X(g)=|\{x\in X: g\cdot x=x\}|$. The function $g\mapsto\fix_X(g)$ is the
permutation character of $X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Burnside mark]
For a finite subgroup $H\le G$,
$\mrk_X(H)=|X^H|=|\{x\in X: h\cdot x = x\ \text{for all } h\in H\}|$. The family
$(\mrk_X(H))_{H\le G}$ is the \emph{mark vector} of $X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Molien invariant]
For a finite subgroup $H\le G$,
$\Mol_X(H) = \frac{1}{|H|}\sum_{h\in H}\fix_X(h)\in\QQ$.
\end{definition}

Two mark vectors are \emph{proportional} if a single $c\in\QQ$ satisfies
$\mrk_X(H)=c\,\mrk_Y(H)$ for all $H$. Note $\mrk_X(\{1\})=\Mol_X(\{1\})=|X|$ and
$\fix_X(1)=|X|$; these trivial-subgroup values pin any hypothetical scalar.

\section{Comparison, equality case, and orbit counting}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:basic}
$\fix_X(1)=|X|$, $\fix_X(g)\le|X|$, $\mrk_X(\{1\})=|X|$ and $\Mol_X(\{1\})=|X|$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the definitions. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:markle}
For every $h\in H$, $\mrk_X(H)\le\fix_X(h)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A point fixed by all of $H$ is fixed by $h$, so $X^H\subseteq X^h$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Structural comparison]\label{thm:cmp}
For every finite subgroup $H\le G$ we have $\mrk_X(H)\le\Mol_X(H)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:markle}, $\sum_{h\in H}\fix_X(h)\ge|H|\,\mrk_X(H)$; divide by $|H|>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:full}
$\mrk_X(H)=|X|$ if and only if $H$ acts trivially on $X$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The mark is $|X^H|$ with $X^H\subseteq X$, so it equals $|X|$ iff $X^H=X$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equality case]\label{thm:eqcase}
$\Mol_X(H)=\mrk_X(H)$ if and only if $H$ acts trivially on $X$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Equality gives $\sum_{h\in H}\fix_X(h)=\sum_{h\in H}\mrk_X(H)$, a sum-wise
equality of two families of natural numbers subject to the termwise inequality of
Lemma~\ref{lem:markle}; hence $\fix_X(h)=\mrk_X(H)$ for each $h\in H$. Taking $h=1$ and using
Lemma~\ref{lem:basic} gives $\mrk_X(H)=|X|$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:full} applies.
($\Leftarrow$) If $H$ acts trivially then $\fix_X(h)=|X|$ for all $h\in H$, so both sides
equal $|X|$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Burnside's lemma, Molien normalisation]\label{thm:burnside}
$\sum_{h\in H}\fix_X(h)=|X/H|\cdot|H|$; equivalently $\Mol_X(H)=|X/H|$, the number of
$H$-orbits.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Double count $\{(h,x)\in H\times X: h\cdot x=x\}$: summing over $h$ gives
$\sum_h\fix_X(h)$; summing over $x$ gives $\sum_x|\mathrm{Stab}_H(x)|$, which contributes
$|H|$ per orbit by the orbit--stabiliser theorem. \end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:int}
$\Mol_X(H)$ is a nonnegative integer.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:dvd}
$|H|$ divides $\sum_{h\in H}\fix_X(h)$; in particular $|G|$ divides $\sum_{g\in G}\fix_X(g)$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Marks determine Molien}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:pow}
If $g\cdot x=x$ then $g^{n}\cdot x = x$ for every $n\in\ZZ$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $n\ge0$ using $g^{n+1}\cdot x=g\cdot(g^{n}\cdot x)$; for $n<0$ apply $g^{-1}$ to
$g\cdot x = x$. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cycfix}
For $g\in G$ with $\langle g\rangle$ finite, $\fix_X(g)=\mrk_X(\langle g\rangle)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$X^{\langle g\rangle}\subseteq X^g$ since $g\in\langle g\rangle$; the reverse inclusion is
Lemma~\ref{lem:pow}. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Averaging Theorem; positive half of D10]\label{thm:avg}
For every finite subgroup $H\le G$,
\[
  \Mol_X(H)\;=\;\frac{1}{|H|}\sum_{h\in H}\mrk_X\bigl(\langle h\rangle\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute Lemma~\ref{lem:cycfix} termwise into the definition of $\Mol_X(H)$. \end{proof}

Thus the Molien invariant is the image of the mark vector under a fixed $\QQ$-linear map
depending only on $G$; and that map evaluates the mark vector only at \emph{cyclic}
subgroups, so every non-cyclic subgroup is structurally invisible to it.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:charmol}
If $\fix_X(g)=\fix_Y(g)$ for all $g\in G$, then $\Mol_X(H)=\Mol_Y(H)$ for every finite
subgroup $H$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The Molien invariant is defined by a formula in the character values alone. \end{proof}

\section{The sharp positive result}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:iso}
If $e\colon X\to Y$ is a bijection with $e(g\cdot x)=g\cdot e(x)$ for all $g,x$, then
$\mrk_X(H)=\mrk_Y(H)$ for every $H$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$e$ restricts to a bijection $X^H\to Y^H$: if $x\in X^H$ then $h\cdot e(x)=e(h\cdot x)=e(x)$;
injectivity of $e$ gives the inverse correspondence. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cyclic Recovery Theorem]\label{thm:cyclic}
If $\fix_X(g)=\fix_Y(g)$ for all $g\in G$, then $\mrk_X(H)=\mrk_Y(H)$ for every cyclic finite
subgroup $H\le G$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $H=\langle g\rangle$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:cycfix},
$\mrk_X(H)=\fix_X(g)=\fix_Y(g)=\mrk_Y(H)$. \end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:cyclic}
If every subgroup of $G$ is cyclic (for instance if $G$ is cyclic), then equal permutation
characters imply equal mark vectors: $\mrk_X(H)=\mrk_Y(H)$ for all $H\le G$, and hence
$X\cong Y$ as $G$-sets. In this case Conjecture~\ref{conj:D10} holds with scaling factor $1$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Refutation: the Klein four group}

Let $V=\ZZ/2\times\ZZ/2$. Its subgroups are the trivial one, three subgroups of order two
(all cyclic), and $V$; the unique non-cyclic subgroup is $V$ itself. The three surjections
onto $\ZZ/2$ are $\chi_0(a,b)=a$, $\chi_1(a,b)=b$, $\chi_2(a,b)=a+b$, with distinct kernels
$A_0,A_1,A_2$.

\begin{definition}
$X_{\mathrm{three}} = V/A_0\sqcup V/A_1\sqcup V/A_2$, concretely $\ZZ/2\times\{0,1,2\}$ with
$g\cdot(t,i)=(t+\chi_i(g),i)$; and $X_{\mathrm{reg}} = V\sqcup\{\bullet_0,\bullet_1\}$, the
regular $V$-set together with two fixed points. Both have six elements.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:kleinchar}
$\fix_{X_{\mathrm{three}}}(g)=\fix_{X_{\mathrm{reg}}}(g)$ for all $g\in V$; both characters are
$(6,2,2,2)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
At $g=1$ both counts are $6$. For $g\neq1$: on $X_{\mathrm{three}}$, $g$ fixes both points of
the $i$-th copy iff $\chi_i(g)=0$, and no point of it otherwise; each nonzero element of $V$
lies in exactly one of $A_0,A_1,A_2$ (the elements $(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)$ lie in $A_1,A_0,A_2$
respectively), so $\fix(g)=2$. On $X_{\mathrm{reg}}$, translation by $g\ne0$ is free on the
regular part and fixes the two added points, so $\fix(g)=2$. \end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:kleinmol}
$\Mol_{X_{\mathrm{three}}}(H)=\Mol_{X_{\mathrm{reg}}}(H)$ for every $H\le V$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:charmol} applied to Lemma~\ref{lem:kleinchar}. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:kleinmarks}
$\mrk_{X_{\mathrm{three}}}(V)=0$, $\mrk_{X_{\mathrm{reg}}}(V)=2$, and
$\mrk_{X_{\mathrm{three}}}(\{1\})=\mrk_{X_{\mathrm{reg}}}(\{1\})=6$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A point $(t,i)$ of $X_{\mathrm{three}}$ is $V$-fixed only if $\chi_i\equiv0$, contradicting
surjectivity of $\chi_i$. In $X_{\mathrm{reg}}$ exactly the two added points are $V$-fixed,
translation being free on the regular part. The marks at $\{1\}$ are the cardinalities.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Conjecture D10 is false]\label{thm:D10false}
The $V$-sets $X_{\mathrm{three}}$ and $X_{\mathrm{reg}}$ have equal Molien invariants at every
subgroup of $V$, yet no $c\in\QQ$ satisfies
$\mrk_{X_{\mathrm{three}}}(H)=c\,\mrk_{X_{\mathrm{reg}}}(H)$ for all $H\le V$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Equality of Molien invariants is Corollary~\ref{cor:kleinmol}. If such a $c$ existed, the
trivial subgroup would give $6=6c$, so $c=1$, and then the top subgroup would give $0=2$, a
contradiction. \end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$X_{\mathrm{three}}$ and $X_{\mathrm{reg}}$ are non-isomorphic $V$-sets with identical
permutation characters; hence the Burnside mark vector is a strictly finer invariant of finite
$G$-sets than the Molien invariant.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
An equivariant bijection would force equal marks at $V$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:iso},
contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:kleinmarks}. \end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:sharp}
$V$ is not cyclic, and the two mark vectors agree at every cyclic subgroup of $V$; hence they
differ exactly at $H=V$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Every element of $V$ squares to the identity, so every cyclic subgroup has order at most
$2<4=|V|$. Agreement on cyclic subgroups is Theorem~\ref{thm:cyclic} applied to
Lemma~\ref{lem:kleinchar}. \end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:sharp} shows that the cyclic hypothesis of Corollary~\ref{cor:cyclic} is
exactly the boundary of the conjecture's validity.

\section{An infinite family: elementary abelian groups of rank two}

Fix a prime $p$ and let $E=(\ZZ/p)^2$, a two-dimensional $\FF_p$-vector space. Its subgroups
of index $p$ are the $p+1$ lines through the origin, the kernels of the functionals indexed by
$\PP^1(\FF_p)=\FF_p\cup\{\infty\}$:
\[
  \chi_i(v_1,v_2)=\begin{cases} v_1+i\,v_2, & i\in\FF_p,\\ v_2, & i=\infty.\end{cases}
\]

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:eaadd}
Each $\chi_i$ is additive and $\chi_i(0)=0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[A nonzero vector lies on exactly one line]\label{lem:lines}
For $v\ne0$ in $\FF_p^2$, $\#\{i\in\PP^1(\FF_p):\chi_i(v)=0\}=1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $v_2=0$ then $v_1\ne0$: $\chi_\infty(v)=0$ while $\chi_c(v)=v_1\ne0$ for $c\in\FF_p$. If
$v_2\ne0$ then $\chi_\infty(v)\ne0$, and $\chi_c(v)=v_1+cv_2=0$ holds for the unique
$c=-v_1/v_2$, division being legitimate in the field $\FF_p$. \end{proof}

\begin{definition}
$X_{\mathrm{lines}}(p)=\bigsqcup_{i\in\PP^1(\FF_p)}E/\ker\chi_i$, concretely
$\FF_p\times\PP^1(\FF_p)$ with $g\cdot(t,i)=(t+\chi_i(g),i)$; and
$X_{\mathrm{reg}}(p)=E\sqcup(p\ \text{fixed points})$. Both have $p(p+1)=p^2+p$ elements.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:eachar}
$\fix_{X_{\mathrm{lines}}(p)}(g)=\fix_{X_{\mathrm{reg}}(p)}(g)$, both equal to $p^2+p$ for
$g=0$ and to $p$ for $g\ne0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
On $X_{\mathrm{lines}}(p)$, translation by $\chi_i(g)$ fixes all $p$ points of the $i$-th copy
if $\chi_i(g)=0$ and none otherwise, so $\fix(g)=p\cdot\#\{i:\chi_i(g)=0\}$, which is
$p(p+1)$ for $g=0$ and $p$ for $g\ne0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:lines}. On $X_{\mathrm{reg}}(p)$,
translation is free on $E$ and the $p$ added points are always fixed. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[D10 fails for every elementary abelian group of rank two]\label{thm:ea}
For every prime $p$ the $E$-sets $X_{\mathrm{lines}}(p)$ and $X_{\mathrm{reg}}(p)$ have equal
Molien invariants at every subgroup of $E$, but no $c\in\QQ$ satisfies
$\mrk_{X_{\mathrm{lines}}(p)}(H)=c\,\mrk_{X_{\mathrm{reg}}(p)}(H)$ for all $H\le E$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Equal characters (Lemma~\ref{lem:eachar}) give equal Molien invariants by
Proposition~\ref{prop:charmol}. At $H=\{1\}$ both marks equal $p^2+p>0$, forcing $c=1$. At
$H=E$: a point $(t,i)$ of $X_{\mathrm{lines}}(p)$ fixed by all of $E$ would force
$\chi_i\equiv0$, impossible, so the mark is $0$; in $X_{\mathrm{reg}}(p)$ exactly the $p$
added points are $E$-fixed, so the mark is $p$. Then $c=1$ demands $0=p$, contradicting
$p\ge2$. \end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:ea} exhibits, for each prime $p$, the relation
$\bigsqcup_{\ell\in\PP^1(\FF_p)}E/\ell \sim E\sqcup p\cdot\pt$ in the kernel of the natural
surjection from the Burnside ring $A(E)$ onto the ring of virtual permutation characters. For
$p=2$ this is the Klein relation of the previous section. The mark difference vector is
supported at the single non-cyclic subgroup $E$, with value $-p$.
\end{remark}

\section{The coarse invariant retains the arithmetic}\label{sec:arith}

\begin{definition}
Let $G$ act on a finite set $Y$ and let $k\ge0$. Put
$\mathrm{Col}(Y,k)=\{f\colon Y\to\{1,\dots,k\}\}$ with $(g\cdot f)(y)=f(g^{-1}\cdot y)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fixcol}
$g\cdot f=f$ if and only if $f$ is constant on each $\langle g\rangle$-orbit of $Y$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Cycle-index identity]\label{thm:cycidx}
With $c(g)=\#\{\langle g\rangle\text{-orbits of }Y\}$, we have
$\fix_{\mathrm{Col}(Y,k)}(g)=k^{\,c(g)}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:fixcol} the $g$-fixed colourings are exactly the functions on the orbit set
$Y/\langle g\rangle$, of which there are $k^{c(g)}$. \end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Frobenius-type congruence]\label{cor:frob}
For any finite group $G$ acting on any finite set $Y$ and any $k\ge0$,
$|G|$ divides $\sum_{g\in G}k^{\,c(g)}$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:dvd} to $X=\mathrm{Col}(Y,k)$ and use Theorem~\ref{thm:cycidx}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:rot}
Let $\ZZ/n$ act on itself by translation. Translation by $a$ generates a subgroup of order
$n/\gcd(n,a)$ acting freely, so $c(a)=\gcd(n,a)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Necklace congruence]\label{thm:necklace}
For all $n\ge1$ and $k\ge0$, $\; n \,\bigm|\, \sum_{a\in\ZZ/n}k^{\gcd(n,a)}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:frob} for $G=Y=\ZZ/n$ with Lemma~\ref{lem:rot}. Equivalently,
$\frac1n\sum_a k^{\gcd(n,a)}$ counts $k$-coloured necklaces with $n$ beads. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:split}
For $p$ prime, $\sum_{a\in\ZZ/p}k^{\gcd(p,a)}=k^{p}+(p-1)k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The term $a=0$ gives $k^{\gcd(p,0)}=k^{p}$; for $0<a<p$ we have $\gcd(p,a)=1$, contributing
$k$ each. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fermat's little theorem]\label{thm:fermat}
For every prime $p$ and every integer $k\ge0$, $\;k^{p}\equiv k \pmod p$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:necklace} and Lemma~\ref{lem:split}, $p$ divides
$k^p+(p-1)k=(k^p-k)+pk$, hence $p\mid k^p-k$. \end{proof}

Only the averaged invariant is used here: the arithmetic content survives the compression that
destroys the structural content.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmx}[Subgroup-indexed invariant tables]
Given a finite group $G$ by its multiplication table and a finite $G$-set $X$: enumerate the
subgroups of $G$ by closing subsets under multiplication; compute $\fix_X(g)$ for each $g$ in
$O(|X|)$; for each subgroup $H$ compute $\mrk_X(H)$ in $O(|H||X|)$ and $\Mol_X(H)$ in
$O(|H|)$. Total cost $O(s|G||X|)$ with $s$ the number of subgroups. The proportionality test
of Conjecture~\ref{conj:D10} fixes $c=|X|/|Y|$ at the trivial subgroup and checks all others.
\end{algorithmx}

\begin{algorithmx}[Orbit counting]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:burnside}, $\Mol_X(H)$ may be computed without averaging, by a union--find
over the generators of $H$ acting on $X$, in near-linear time. Agreement of the two
computations is a numerical confirmation of Burnside's lemma.
\end{algorithmx}

\begin{algorithmx}[Necklace counting]
Compute $N(n,k)=\frac1n\sum_{a=0}^{n-1}k^{\gcd(n,a)}=\frac1n\sum_{d\mid n}\varphi(n/d)k^{d}$.
Integrality for all inputs is Theorem~\ref{thm:necklace}; at $n=p$ prime it is
Theorem~\ref{thm:fermat}.
\end{algorithmx}

\section{Discussion}

The Burnside ring $A(G)$ has the mark homomorphism as a faithful coordinate system, and the
permutation character defines a ring homomorphism $A(G)\to R(G)$ into the representation ring.
Theorem~\ref{thm:avg} identifies this homomorphism at the level of coordinates: the character
is the restriction of the mark vector to the cyclic part of the subgroup poset, averaged.
Conjecture~\ref{conj:D10} asks whether that restriction is injective up to scaling, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:D10false} answers no as soon as a non-cyclic subgroup is present in a suitable
position.

The ``modulo scaling'' clause is a red herring: the trivial subgroup pins $c=|X|/|Y|$, and
since equality of Molien invariants already forces $|X|=|Y|$ (both equal the value at the
trivial subgroup), one always has $c=1$. Corollary~\ref{cor:cyclic} and
Proposition~\ref{prop:sharp} sandwich the truth, and the expected exact dichotomy is
Conjecture C1 below.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{C1. The cyclic dichotomy is exact.} For a finite group $G$ the following should be
equivalent: (i) any two finite $G$-sets with equal permutation characters have equal Burnside
mark vectors; (ii) every subgroup of $G$ is cyclic. A failure of injectivity of ``mark vector
$\mapsto$ character'' is forced by a single non-cyclic section: the $(\ZZ/p)^2$ family above
shows that a rank-two elementary abelian subquotient already produces a character-equal,
mark-different pair, and by Cauchy's theorem a non-cyclic finite abelian group contains such a
subgroup; the general case should follow by inducing the pair up along $G/H$. Direction
(ii)$\Rightarrow$(i) and the elementary abelian instances of (i)$\Rightarrow$(ii) are settled
here; only the induction step -- a finite, purely combinatorial construction -- is missing.

\paragraph{C2. Quantitative defect: the corank of the character map.} The kernel of the map
``Burnside mark vector $\mapsto$ permutation character'' should have rank equal to the number
of conjugacy classes of subgroups of $G$ minus the number of conjugacy classes of cyclic
subgroups; for $(\ZZ/p)^2$ this is exactly $1$, matching the single relation
$\bigsqcup_\ell E/\ell\sim E\sqcup p\cdot\pt$ proved above. The mark matrix is triangular with
respect to the subgroup order, so the character map factors through the cyclic part of the
table of marks and the defect is an index count on the subgroup lattice rather than a
representation-theoretic computation.

\paragraph{C3. Graded Molien series versus marks.} The graded Molien series
$M_X(t)=\frac{1}{|G|}\sum_g\prod_{\text{cycles }c\text{ of }g}(1-t^{|c|})^{-1}$ refines the
constant-term data studied here, recording the full cycle type of each element. How much of
the mark vector does it recover, and does the cycle-type refinement already separate the
$(\ZZ/p)^2$ pair?

\paragraph{C4. Arithmetic consequences beyond necklaces.} Any family of actions with a
computable cycle index yields a congruence family; natural targets are Gauss's congruence
$\sum_{d\mid n}\mu(n/d)k^{d}\equiv0\pmod n$, analogues for non-cyclic groups, and congruences
from actions on colourings of higher-dimensional grids.

\section{Conclusion}

Conjecture D10 is false. The Molien invariant is always a linear image of the mark vector --
at $H$ it is the average of the marks at the cyclic subgroups generated by the elements of $H$
-- and conversely determines the mark vector exactly when every subgroup is cyclic. For the
Klein four group there are two six-element $G$-sets with identical Molien invariants at every
subgroup whose mark vectors are non-proportional, and the same holds over $(\ZZ/p)^2$ for
every prime $p$, with sets of size $p^2+p$; the mark vectors differ only at non-cyclic
subgroups. Nevertheless $|H|$ divides $\sum_{h\in H}|X^h|$, and applying this to colouring
actions of $\ZZ/n$ produces the necklace congruence and Fermat's little theorem. Averaging
over a group sees only its cyclic shadows: enough for counting, not enough for classification.

\end{document}

